WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.209
On Nov 4th 2019, former boy band member and
current fashion icon, Harry Styles, posted

00:00:06.209 --> 00:00:07.649
an image to twitter.

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:10.620
Within 24 hours it racked up over half a million
likes.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.170
It was the cover to his long awaited sophomore
album, and fans dissected every last detail.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.670
From the pink and blue color palette,
which was used for the studio backdrop, his

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.430
custom Gucci outfit, and was even painted
on his fingernails.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:29.179
To this disembodied hand in the corner, which
belongs to Tim Walker, the photographer.

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:33.860
And finally, the scandalous release date - Friday
December 13th.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.649
His ex-girlfriend, Taylor Swift’s, birthday.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:39.850
But zoom out to the whole image, and you’ll
see something that extends far beyond the

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.480
world of Harry Styles fandom.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.160
This circular photograph is the latest in
a long line of album covers that have that

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.860
same warped composition.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.270
This is the story of music’s obsession with
the fisheye lens.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:04.370
In 1906 renowned physicist Robert W. Wood
was in his lab at Johns Hopkins University

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:10.050
with a bucket full of water, a pinhole camera,
mirrored glass, and a lot of light.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:15.720
He wanted to see if he could create an image
of the world from the perspective of a fish.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.970
Whose view from underwater compresses the
entire horizon.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.130
This experiment might seem unusual, but Woods
was a professor of optical physics and he

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:30.170
dedicated his career to inventing unconventional
tools to study light.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.720
From developing this disk whose microscopic
components helped determine the age of stars,

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.470
to inventing this toy which, according to
the patent, “created grotesque images of

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:45.830
people’s faces” through a series of perfectly
placed mirrors.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:51.270
I first heard of him as being sort of an offbeat
kind of guy.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.720
He often had a deep insight into things and
kind of a quirky way.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.980
That’s John Beaver, a photographer who is
also a Professor of Physics and Astronomy.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.880
That basic diagram of the rays coming at different
angles, I teach that all the time, it’s

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.310
in every beginning physics book.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:14.360
But the idea of making a camera out of that,
I had never heard that Robert Wood had done

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.360
that.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.000
In this paper, Wood detailed what he wanted
to see:

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.620
“the circular picture would contain everything
embraced within an angle of 180 degress in

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.790
every direction, i.e. a complete hemisphere.”

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.040
And luckily, he left us with a set of instructions.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.140
Diameter of the paper, what’s that?

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.820
Open only in photography darkroom.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.360
I feel like I’m about to murder somebody.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.720
Let’s go get some water.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.280
Anchors away.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.560
Let’s get another one of those clamps.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.980
In case of contact flash skin or eyes with
water for at least 15 minutes.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.420
That’s fine.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.900
Oh no it’s not because it has to be under
the pinhole.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.920
I honestly have no idea how the hell he did
this.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.020
I think there's an easy way for you to do it now that he couldn't have done.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.780
You can just put a waterproof
GoPro in the bottom of the bucket.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:12.800
God dammit.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.880
Can we just cut to the pictures?

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.700
This is a picture he took from under a bridge.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.370
This is a line up of men.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.020
And this is what a fish might see if all those
men were looking down at it from above the

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.740
surface of a pond.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.880
Soon, researchers and scientists built on
Wood’s idea.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.010
From 1915 through the 1930s, it was the fields
of meteorology and astronomy that drove the

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.660
development of a camera lens that could capture
that coveted fisheye perspective.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:45.160
And on the eve of World War II, German inventors
filed a patent for a lens which they then

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.890
shared with the Japanese company Nikon.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:51.120
The lens used the same principles as Woods’
water experiment.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.420
You can see it right in his diagram with the bucket.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.960
As the light rays come in at a steep angle,
they come out at a less steep angle.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:05.040
And so those light rays that are coming from
huge angles are compressed onto the picture.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.850
In 1957 Nikon produced their first special
order fish-eye camera, factoring in inflation,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.470
it was a $27,000 piece of gear.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.280
Though they were still primarily used for
scientific research, a few caught the attention

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.340
of magazine and newspaper photographers.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.290
This 1957 issue of Life Magazine, shows a
fisheye view of a senate hearing.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:30.900
A few months later that same camera photographed
a pole vaulter mid jump.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:36.210
And in 1962, Sixty years after Robert Woods’
original experiments, Nikon’s first

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.020
consumer grade fish-eye lens hit the market.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.190
And it became a pop culture phenomenon.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:45.960
It captured baseball stadiums
Shark cages

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.270
Political conventions
The Apollo trainings

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.320
And protests.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.680
It was and always has been a handy tool to
capture tight quarters.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.350
as well as huge spaces.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:06.070
But perhaps its greatest strength was making
rock stars appear larger than life.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.830
When the Beatles kicked off the British Invasion
in the mid 60s.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:13.930
The fish eye lens was uniquely suited to document
the insanity.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.530
Here they are at a Miami press conference.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:20.020
And during a TV performance.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:24.220
The len’s warped perspective reflected the
trippiness of the psychedelic era.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:33.100
Including the Woodstock music festival.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:38.199
This is jazz musician, Sam Rivers’, 1965
album Fuchsia Swing Song and it’s one of

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:40.510
the earliest fisheye album covers.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:43.740
A few months later The Byrds Released Mr Tambourine
Man.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.680
Over the next few years the fisheye album
art format cemented itself in music.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.080
The most common format was this:
A giant circular fisheye lens portrait of

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:57.440
the artist or band.
with typography above and below it.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.770
In more illustrated approaches, the font wraps
fully around the image

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.780
And some exclude the artist altogether, opting
to show an interesting scene or landscape.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.690
Oh, I just thought of a fish eye album cover.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:13.490
Do you know Hergest Ridge by Mike Oldfield?

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:18.300
No, but I’m going to look it up.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.110
Wow that’s cool.

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.230
The fisheye lens was a go-to piece of gear
for music photographers and filmmakers by

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.090
the end of the 1970s.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:32.820
It was there for concerts, television performances,
and photoshoots.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:40.199
So by the time MTV launched in 1981, it was
inevitable that the super wide angle lens

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.210
would play a huge role in music videos.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:52.070
It could get super close to a performer while
still capturing the space around them.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.750
This is the music video for the Beastie Boys
track “Shake your Rump”

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:59.030
It was shot with at least three cameras equipped
with a fisheye lens.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:03.420
Fisheye worked for every genre, from 60s hippies
to alt rock to hip hop.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.780
But hip-hop had a sense of humor, and it was
in your face.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.630
Which helped turn it into one of the genre’s
signature looks.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.080
And in the 90s that's thanks to director Hype Williams.
—

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:20.240
From 1991 to 1999 he directed dozens of music video for top artists.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.720
And many of those prominently featured a fisheye perspective.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.100
Busta Rhymes,

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:27.340
TLC,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.260
and of course, Missy Elliot.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.000
The list goes on and on.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.600
And let's not forget those album covers.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.520
The Fisheye lens is, no doubt, a novelty.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:45.490
When you see a photo taken with it, your eye
will likely first focus on the highly distorted

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:50.370
style of the image, and then register what’s
in it.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:54.490
But that undersells how timeless and useful
of a tool it actually is.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.540
One generation sees it as a psychedelic photography
trope.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:00.470
Another a 1990s hip-hop throwback.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:05.120
If you’re an astronomer, it helps you track
clouds moving across the atmosphere

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.690
and the terrain on the surface of Mars.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:14.980
And if you’re as curious as Robert Woods,
it’ll let you see the world from the point

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:24.390
of view of 
a fish.

